The ORCA half-plane construction and the three-stage linear program in `src/lib.rs`
follow that implementation closely enough that its identifiers are deliberately kept —
`linear_program1` / `linear_program2` / `linear_program3`, `t_left` / `t_right`, and
`RVO_EPSILON` (here `EPSILON`) — so the two can be read side by side.

Changed from RVO2: every half-plane is stored at its point nearest the origin, and
`linear_program3` builds each projected line from the two lines' distances to the origin
rather than through their intersection, so the answer stays inside the speed disc to `f32`
rounding (RVO2's can leave it by many times `maxSpeed`). The half-planes and the three
stages are RVO2's.

The obstacle lines in `src/obstacles.rs` are RVO2's too: the obstacle branch of
`Agent::computeNewVelocity` (cutoff lines and circles round the ends of an edge, legs, the
"foreign" leg rule, the already-covered test) case for case, with its identifiers kept
(`left_leg_direction`, `is_left_leg_foreign`, `left_cutoff`, `t_left` / `t_right`,
`dist_sq_cutoff`), and the per-vertex data of `Obstacle` and `RVOSimulator::addObstacle`
(the unit direction of the edge, the convex flag, the previous and next vertex).

Changed from RVO2: a polygon is given by what is solid (`add_solid`, `add_enclosure`) and
oriented by the builder, where RVO2 asks for counter-clockwise vertices and silently ignores
a clockwise one; edges are found by a uniform grid, where RVO2's kd-tree splits an obstacle at
another's line, so the lines an agent gets there depend on how the tree happened to
partition; a wall with no thickness has no "foreign" legs, because its neighbouring edge is
its own other side, which RVO2's rule skips and an agent touching the wall's face can then
be handed no line at all; and a zero-length direction RVO2 would normalise to NaN is
replaced by a unit vector.

